{
  "dom_id": "step-e154",
  "name": "Example: Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem",
  "problem": "An oil tank has leaked $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours. The graph of $q(t)$ is shown below. Approximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/b6c5286dae7e739a588173bad9c721ae34d07f86e455e44ec9f229628cf54211.png)",
  "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\nq'(t) \\approx \\frac{q(t+h) - q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{\u2032}(1) & \u2248\\frac{q(1+1)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ hour, the tank is leaking oil at a rate of approximately $1\\,\\textrm{liter}/ \\textrm{hour}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
